---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../../components/Comments.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="My First Mission Trip: Esports in Guadalajara - Caleb McGennis"
  description="My first mission trip. Sports Crusaders took an esports camp to Guadalajara, Mexico - 25 kids, a broken line array, six translators, and a tournament won by a kid who had never been the good one at anything."
  type="article"
  publishedTime="2026-08-25"
  author="Caleb Michael McGennis"
  image="/images/social/first-mission-trip-guadalajara-esports-og.jpg"
>
  <!-- Hero Section -->
  <section class="bg-soho-white py-20 md:py-32 px-4 border-b border-gray-200">
    <div class="container mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <a href="/blog/entrepreneur-ai" class="inline-flex items-center text-mcgennis-navy hover:text-mcgennis-gold transition-colors mb-8">
        <svg class="w-5 h-5 mr-2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"/>
        </svg>
        Back to An Entrepreneur in a World of AI
      </a>
      <h1 class="text-5xl md:text-6xl font-display font-bold mb-8 text-gray-900 leading-tight tracking-tight">
        My First Mission Trip
      </h1>
      <div class="h-1 w-24 bg-brass-horizontal rounded-full shadow-sm mb-8"></div>
      <p class="text-2xl text-gray-500 font-light mb-8">Guadalajara, twenty-five kids, and efficient impact.</p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-gray-500">
        <span>By Caleb Michael McGennis</span>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <time datetime="2026-08-25">August 25, 2026</time>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Article Content -->
  <article class="py-16 px-4">
    <div class="container mx-auto max-w-3xl prose prose-lg">
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8 font-light">
        This was my first mission trip. Third time I'd ever left the country. And I spent it doing the thing I already do every week at home &mdash; setting up consoles, running a bracket, and talking to kids about Jesus in between matches.
      </p>


      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">What We Actually Do</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I run esports events for <a href="https://sportscrusaders.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-mcgennis-navy hover:text-mcgennis-gold transition-colors underline">Sports Crusaders</a>, a non-profit whose whole mission is bringing the gospel to people through common ground. We've been doing the esports side for five years now &mdash; Super Summers, DNows, youth groups. We show up, we set up, we run a tournament or just open free play, and we talk about Jesus with kids who came for the games.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The premise is simple. Sports ministry has always worked because a basketball is an excuse to be in a kid's life. A controller works exactly the same way, for exactly the kids a basketball could never reach.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Four of us went: me, Josh Arnold, and our two interns, Gabe Adams and Adam Berck.
      </p>

      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/trip/gdl-team-sports-crusaders-four.webp" alt="The Sports Crusaders esports team in Guadalajara" class="w-full" />
        <div class="p-4 bg-soho-white">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">The four of us, plus the people who made the week work</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Saturday, Then Sunday</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We flew out on a Saturday and landed Saturday night, which meant our first real morning in Mexico was a Sunday morning, which meant church.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I gave my testimony. A shorter, more general version of <a href="/testimony/caleb" class="text-mcgennis-navy hover:text-mcgennis-gold transition-colors underline">the one I've written down here</a> &mdash; you learn quickly that a testimony delivered through a translator needs to be built in smaller pieces. Afterward people came up to us for a long time. Encouraging us. Thanking us. We hadn't done anything yet. They thanked us anyway.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Then they fed us, at a long table in the fellowship hall, with more horchata than four Missourians knew what to do with.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8">
        <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/trip/gdl-sunday-service-predicacion.webp" alt="Sunday service at the church in Guadalajara" class="w-full h-72 object-cover" />
          <div class="p-3 bg-soho-white"><p class="text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">Sunday morning &mdash; where I gave my testimony</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/trip/gdl-horchata-toast-welcome-meal.webp" alt="Long table in the fellowship hall, glasses of horchata raised in a toast" class="w-full h-72 object-cover" />
          <div class="p-3 bg-soho-white"><p class="text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">The welcome meal. Glasses up.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Twenty-Five Kids</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We had twenty-five kids at camp.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Here's the thing about our kids, and it's true at every single camp we run: they're shy. That's the population. The kid who's home in his room playing games instead of at practice is not usually the kid who walks into a room full of strangers and introduces himself. Getting them to open up is most of the job.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        In Guadalajara there was a wrinkle we hadn't planned for. A lot of these families didn't have much, and consoles and games are genuinely expensive in Mexico. So we had a room full of kids who were <em>familiar</em> with video games &mdash; they knew the characters, they knew the games by name &mdash; but a lot of them had never really gotten to play one.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        One kid asked me whether the tournament winner got a prize. I said we'd see. He said, "like a Switch?" I told him no. He thought about it for a second and countered: "I'll give you fifty pesos for one."
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Fifty pesos is about three dollars. I have not stopped telling that story since.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8">
        <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/trip/gdl-salon-door-controller.webp" alt="A classroom door decorated with a game controller and a mushroom" class="w-full h-72 object-cover" />
          <div class="p-3 bg-soho-white"><p class="text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">Where we shared the gospel.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/trip/gdl-esports-station-setup.webp" alt="Console stations set up on blue tablecloths" class="w-full h-72 object-cover" />
          <div class="p-3 bg-soho-white"><p class="text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">Setup day &mdash; stations on blue tablecloths</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">The Kid Who Was Never the Good One</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Sports Crusaders had been to this church before &mdash; sports camps, in the years leading up to esports. So some of these kids had done this already &mdash; a week of American guys showing up with a sport.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        One of ours had been to all of them. And on the last day, one of the translators pulled me aside to tell me what his mother had told her.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        He'd get frustrated, because he was never the most coordinated. In a gym the fastest and the tallest and the strongest are the only three things that get you noticed, and he was none of the three. He'd spent years being the kid who was there. He had no real reason to be excited about another camp.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Then we showed up with Smash.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        He opened up over the week, and I'll be honest, we thought that was mostly us. We joked with him a lot. He gave it back. He started challenging me every chance he got &mdash; walking up between rounds looking for a match, every single day. And as he learned the game, he was genuinely good at it.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        But it was his mom's version that got me. She said his whole attitude had changed. He was waking up early on his own. He was rushing <em>her</em> out the door so they'd get there faster. He was talking more. He was making friends.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        On Friday he played through the entire bracket and won the whole thing.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8">
        <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/trip/gdl-tournament-winners.webp" alt="Tournament winners holding their prizes with the Sports Crusaders team" class="w-full h-80 object-cover" />
          <div class="p-3 bg-soho-white"><p class="text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">The winners, holding what they won</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
          <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/trip/gdl-mario-kart-prize-box.webp" alt="A boxed copy of Mario Kart bought as a tournament prize" class="w-full h-80 object-cover" />
          <div class="p-3 bg-soho-white"><p class="text-xs text-gray-600 font-light">Thursday prize run &mdash; we wanted the ending to feel special</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We'd gone out Thursday and bought prizes, because we wanted the ending to feel special. Watching a kid who had never once been the good one at anything walk up and collect first place &mdash; I don't have a better sentence for it than that.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We get a story like that at every camp. It has never once stopped landing.
      </p>

      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/trip/gdl-campers-at-stations.webp" alt="Campers playing at the console stations" class="w-full" />
        <div class="p-4 bg-soho-white">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Twenty-five kids, most of whom had never owned a console</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">The Right Line Array</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We'd noticed it on Sunday. The right line array had no highs &mdash; lows and mids were there, the top end just wasn't &mdash; and once you've spent five years running audio for a church you cannot un-notice something like that. It doesn't read as broken to most people in the room. It reads as dull.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        A few days later we had a free afternoon, so Josh and I found Antonio. He's the lead pastor there, and he was our contact for the whole trip. He didn't have to care about a dead tweeter on the right side of his room, and he came and stood in a torn-apart rack with us for an afternoon anyway.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We started at the board and worked forward. Signal to the processor. Processor to the amps. They're running the system three-way &mdash; separate amps for lows, mids, and highs &mdash; so every one had to be verified on its own before we could rule anything out. Confirm signal in. Confirm signal out. Move to the next box. Then do it again, because the first pass is always a lie.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Thirty minutes in, we were fine. An hour in, we were quieter. An hour and a half in, I started doing the math on leaving a church worse off than we found it, with the rack pulled apart and a Sunday coming.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        This is going to sound small if you've never done it. But standing in that room, Josh and I both felt the same thing: we were about to be the guys who flew to Guadalajara to play video games and couldn't fix a speaker. We wanted this church to see we were good for more than a tournament bracket.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        So we prayed about it. Right there, in the middle of a torn-apart rack.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        And then we found it. The highs amp.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The cables were unplugged.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Ninety minutes. Three amps verified one at a time, twice. Two guys who do this for a living, and the lead pastor of the church standing there with us the whole time. And the answer was sitting at the end of the chain &mdash; the highs simply weren't connected to anything.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I've thought about that more than I expected to. We prayed for help finding something complicated and what we got was the simplest possible answer, right after we'd finally slowed down enough to look at it. Make of that what you want. I know what I make of it.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We brought the array back up and then stayed to actually mix it. Not just working &mdash; balanced, subs through highs, the whole room. Antonio heard his own sanctuary at full range, and I don't think he'd expected to that week.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Then I built them an EQ.
      </p>

      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/trip/gdl-mixer-eq-curve.webp" alt="A digital mixing console showing a four-band parametric EQ curve on AUX1" class="w-full" />
        <div class="p-4 bg-soho-white">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">AUX1, four bands, saved and left behind</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Four bands of parametric on AUX1: about five decibels of lift at 49 Hz to give the low end somewhere to sit,
        two and a half out at 195 to clear the mud, another two and three-quarters off the high mids at 1.44k where a
        hard room like that one turns harsh, and a narrow five-decibel notch up at 11.9k.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I didn't build that for us. I built it for their audio engineer &mdash; the media director who runs that room
        every Sunday, fifty-one weeks a year when there are no Americans in the building. Reconnecting a cable is a
        favor. A tuned starting point he can keep working from is something he actually gets to keep.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Nobody Came to Faith</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I want to be straight about this, because mission trip write-ups have a habit of ending with a number.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Twenty-five kids. Not one profession of faith that week.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I'd be lying if I said that didn't sit heavy on the flight home. You go, you pour out everything you have for a week, and the column you most wanted to fill reads zero.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        But common ground is the entire strategy, and common ground is slow on purpose. A kid who spent a week being known, being joked with, being good at something for the first time in his life &mdash; and who heard the gospel from people who had earned the right to share it with them &mdash; is not a kid nothing happened to. That's a seed in a church that is still there, with a pastor who is still there, and translators who are still there long after our flight left.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We don't get to set the timeline. We just get to go.
      </p>

      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
        <img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/images/trip/gdl-team-hands-in-huddle.webp" alt="Hands stacked together in a team huddle" class="w-full" />
        <div class="p-4 bg-soho-white">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Hands in</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-mcgennis-navy text-white rounded-xl p-8 my-12">
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold mb-4 tracking-tight">To Everyone Who Sent Us</h3>
        <p class="mb-4">
          None of this happens without the people who support Sports Crusaders and who supported this trip specifically.
          You bought the prizes on Thursday. You put four guys and a pile of consoles in Guadalajara for a week.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm opacity-90">
          Thank you. Genuinely.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <Comments />

